# How ReSTIR Lights a Room of Many Lights {#video subtitle="Bitterli et al. 2020, spatiotemporal reservoir resampling, implemented from the paper" date=2026-11-12 fps=30 width=1920 height=1080}

## The paper and its problem {#c1 number=1 target=245 summary="The paper, the problem it solves, how it solves it, and its contributions." sources="problem, approach, reservoir-intro, pdfs-not-colors, biased-mode, first-vertex"}

### The paper {#c1-paper kind=2d diagram=paper}

${cue "key" {' {callout "problem"}}}This video is about a paper by Benedikt Bitterli, Chris Wyman, Matt Pharr, Peter Shirley, Aaron Lefohn, and Wojciech Jarosz.

The paper is called Spatiotemporal reservoir resampling for real-time ray tracing with dynamic direct lighting.

It was published in ACM Transactions on Graphics in 2020.

### Sixty-four moving lights {#c1-problem kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReuse panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {append
    {noReuse 1.0}
    {' {restirSeed 5.0} {lightCount 1.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${overText {' {lightCountRange 1.0 12.0}} "The paper's problem shows up in a room like this one"}.

It is lit by ${overText {' {lightCountRange 12.0 64.0}} "sixty-four small lights"}, and ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.04}}
    "all of them move"}.

Every point on the floor and the walls ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.04 0.08}}
    "receives some light from each of them"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "first-vertex"}}}To know whether a light reaches a point, a renderer traces ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.08 0.12}}
    "a shadow ray from the point to the light"}.

In real time, each pixel can afford only a few of those rays.

So each pixel has to guess ${overText {' {restirSeedRange 5.0 7.0}} "which lights are worth a ray"}.

${overText {' {restirSeedRange 7.0 11.0}} "Here each pixel guesses by picking one light at random"}, favouring the brighter ones.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 4.2 sentence}}}Most of those guesses ${overText
    {' {restirSeedRange 11.0 15.0}}
    "land on lights that add little"} to that pixel.

That is where all this noise comes from.

${cue "key" {' {callout "problem"}}}So the paper asks how each pixel can find the lights that matter to it, ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.12 0.2}}
    "even while they move"}, within a real-time frame.

### The answer, in three steps {#c1-solution kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {append
    {noReuse 1.0}
    {' {restirStage 3.0} {restirVisibilityReuse 0.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "approach"}}}Its answer starts by letting each pixel make ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 1.0 8.0}}
    "many cheap guesses instead of one"}.

For each guess, the pixel estimates how much light it would bring, leaving shadows out.

That estimate is cheap, because it needs no ray.

The pixel then ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 8.0 32.0}}
    "keeps one guess, chosen in proportion to its estimate"}.

${overText
    {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "Next, each pixel borrows the guesses its neighbours kept"}.

${overText
    {' {restirTemporalRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "It also borrows the guess its point kept in the previous frame"}.

Borrowing helps, because ${overText {' {restirNeighboursRange 5.0 8.0}} "nearby pixels"} and nearby moments usually need the same lights.

${cue "key" {' {callout "reservoir-intro"}}}Only the guesses a pixel finally keeps ${overText
    {' {restirVisibilityReuseRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "cost shadow rays"}, so the rays per pixel stay few.

${cue "key" {' {callout "pdfs-not-colors"}}}${overText
    {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "Sharing between pixels"} is not new in itself.

A denoiser shares colours between pixels.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 1.0}}}This method instead shares the guesses each pixel kept, ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.05}}
    "which say which lights to pick"}.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 0.0}}}Sharing that, rather than colours, is what ${overText
    {' {restirUnbiasedRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "makes an unbiased version possible"}, one that is correct on average.

${cue "key" {' {callout "biased-mode"}}}The paper also gives a biased version, which ${overText
    {' {restirUnbiasedRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "reaches the same quality faster"}.

But the biased version darkens the edges of objects a little.

### Four contributions {#c1-contributions kind=2d diagram=contributions}

${cue "key" {' {callout "name"}}}The paper calls this method ReSTIR, short for reservoir-based spatiotemporal importance resampling.

This video groups the method's ideas into four contributions.

### Streaming {#c1-c-stream kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRis panelHidden {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {append
    {noReuse 8.0}
    {' {restirSeed 5.0} {risStep 0.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {callout "approach"}}}First, a pixel does not keep a list of its guesses, ${overText
    {' {risStepRange 0.0 8.0}}
    "only one tiny record"}.

The paper calls that record a reservoir.

### Reuse {#c1-c-reuse kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpatial panelHidden {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {'
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 1.0}
    {restirNeighbours 1.0}
    {restirSeed 5.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

Second, reservoirs can be combined, ${overText
    {' {restirNeighboursRange 1.0 5.0}}
    "across neighbouring pixels"} and across frames.

### No bias {#c1-c-unbiased kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom roomCamera}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "pdfs-not-colors"}}}Third, the paper shows where that combining introduces bias.

It also gives ${overText {' {restirUnbiasedRange 0.0 1.0}} "a weight that removes the bias"}.

### On the GPU {#c1-c-gpu kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    {restirUnbiased 1.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "biased-mode"}}}Fourth, the method ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.04}}
    "runs on the GPU in real time"}.

It comes in ${overText {' {restirUnbiasedRange 1.0 0.0}} "a biased and an unbiased form"}.

### The passes of a frame {#c1-map kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    {restirStage 0.0}
    {restirCandidates 1.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

On the GPU, each frame runs the method as a chain of passes, ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.04}}
    "steps that each run over all the pixels"}.

In order, each pixel ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 1.0 32.0}}
    "draws its guesses and keeps one"}.

It ${overText {' {restirStageRange 0.0 1.0}} "tests that kept guess with a shadow ray"}.

Then it ${overText {' {restirStageRange 1.0 2.0}} "borrows from the previous frame"} and ${overText
    {' {restirStageRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "from its neighbours"}, and is shaded.

### Last frame feeds the next {#c1-map-loop kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReuse panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {'
    {restirSpatialPasses 0.0}
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirView 2.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

Each frame keeps the reservoirs it ends with.

The next frame ${overText {' {restirTemporalRange 0.0 1.0}} "borrows from them"}, so the frames form a loop.

### What this story teaches {#c1-teach kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    {restirStage 0.0}
    {restirCandidates 1.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

By the end, you will be able to ${overText
    {' {restirStageRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "explain each of those passes"}, and why their result stays correct.

On the way, the video teaches the two older ideas the method is built from.

They are resampled importance sampling, ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 1.0 32.0}}
    "which picks one guess from many"}, and weighted reservoir sampling, which keeps that pick in a tiny record.

The video assumes you know Monte Carlo integration with importance sampling, and how a physically based renderer shades a surface.

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.05}} "Every demonstration is rendered by the lab"}, the program that made this video.

The lab implements the paper's algorithms from its text.

### Where to read it {#c1-sources kind=2d sources="problem, approach, reservoir-intro, pdfs-not-colors, biased-mode, first-vertex"}

This chapter follows the paper's introduction, on its first two pages.

## Related work {#c2 number=2 target=425 summary="What ReSTIR replaces, and the two ideas it is built from: resampled importance sampling and weighted reservoir sampling." sources="many-lights, hierarchies, tiled, path-reuse, denoisers, eq3, eq4, eq5, eq6, ris-unbiased, ris-converges, wrs, eq9, alg2"}

### Other ways to light many lights {#c2-others kind=2d diagram=related focus=OTHERS}

Before the two older ideas this video teaches, a look at what else has been tried.

The paper discusses this related work at the end, in section seven, but it helps to meet it first.

### Light trees {#c2-many kind=2d diagram=related focus=MANY}

${cue "key" {' {callout "many-lights"}}}One family of methods sorts the lights by how much they matter, often into a tree that can be sampled quickly.

${cue "key" {' {callout "hierarchies"}}}But a tree has to be updated whenever its lights move.

A real-time frame has little time for that.

### Tiles {#c2-tiled kind=2d diagram=related focus=TILED}

${cue "key" {' {callout "tiled"}}}Another family, tiled shading, keeps a short list of lights for each tile of the screen.

To keep each list short, it usually limits how far each light reaches.

That limit biases the image, which means the image is wrong on average.

### Shared paths {#c2-paths kind=2d diagram=related focus=PATHS}

${cue "key" {' {callout "path-reuse"}}}Path reuse shares, between pixels, the paths light takes from a light to the camera.

But it traces a new shadow ray every time it reuses a path.

### Denoisers {#c2-denoise kind=2d diagram=related focus=DENOISE}

${cue "key" {' {callout "denoisers"}}}Denoisers blur the noisy colours after rendering.

ReSTIR does not replace them, but gives them better samples to start from.

### Two foundations {#c2-foundations kind=2d diagram=related focus="FOUNDATIONS, RESTIR"}

ReSTIR itself is built from two older ideas, resampled importance sampling and weighted reservoir sampling.

### Importance sampling, recalled {#c2-is kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRis panelHidden {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {append
    {noReuse 1.0}
    {' {restirSeed 1.0} {risStep 1.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq3"}}}The first of these ideas starts from importance sampling.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}${overText
    {' {pickURange 0.84 0.88}}
    "Take one pixel, P"}, on the floor by the right wall.

To estimate the light at P, the renderer ${overText
    {' {restirSeedRange 1.0 5.0}}
    "draws one light sample"}, a point on one of the lights.

The estimate is ${formulaSaid f"{\frac{f(x)}{p(x)}}" "what that sample brings, f, divided by the probability p it was drawn with"
    {'}}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 22.0 sentence}}}Dividing by p balances the samples, because ${overText
    {' {restirSeedRange 5.0 17.0}}
    "a likely sample is drawn often"} and so must count less.

The closer p follows what each sample brings, the less noise is left.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq4"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.3 sentence}}}One more idea from importance sampling comes back later in the paper.

Multiple importance sampling mixes ${overText
    {' {restirSeedRange 17.0 21.0}}
    "several ways of drawing samples"}, with weights that sum to one.

### What we would like to sample {#c2-ris-question kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRis panelHidden {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {append
    {noReuse 1.0}
    {' {restirSeed 5.0} {risStep 1.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq5"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}So for a single p, the best choice would follow ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.03}}
    "what each light sample brings to P"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 24.0 sentence}}}What a sample brings depends on ${overText
    {' {lightIntensityRange 1.0 1.5}}
    "the light's emission"}, on P's surface, and on ${overText
        {' {lightPhaseRange 0.03 0.07}}
        "the angle and distance between the light and P"}.

The paper calls that ideal p the target.

It writes the target as p-hat.

The target need not add up to one, so it is not quite a probability.

${overText
    {' {restirSeedRange 5.0 9.0}}
    "But no one can draw samples in exact proportion to the target"}.

${overText
    {' {restirSeedRange 9.0 13.0}}
    "Instead, a cheap choice draws lights in proportion to their power"}, and ignores P altogether.

That cheap choice is the lab's starting point.

### Resampled importance sampling {#c2-ris kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRis panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {append
    {noReuse 8.0}
    {' {restirSeed 5.0} {risStep 0.0} {risWeigh 0.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq5"}}}Resampled importance sampling, RIS for short, gets from the cheap choice to the target in two steps.

${overText {' {risStepRange 0.0 8.0}} "First, P draws eight samples the cheap way"}.

Each sample is a light and a point on it, one of the guesses from chapter one.

These samples are called candidates.

The cheap way favours the candidates on the brightest lights.

So those candidates turn up often, whatever they bring to P.

To take away that head start, ${overText {' {risWeighRange 0.0 1.0}} "each candidate gets a weight"}.

The weight is ${formulaSaid f"{w = \frac{\hat{p}(x)}{p(x)}}" "its target divided by the probability it was drawn with"
    {'}}.

A candidate drawn often is divided by a large probability, so each time it turns up, it counts for less.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}Here ${overText
    {' {pickURange 0.88 0.9}}
    "the brighter a line from P"}, the larger its candidate's weight.

The panel lists each candidate's target, the probability it was drawn with, and the weight between them.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 24.0 sentence}}}Second, P keeps one candidate, ${overText
    {' {risPickRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "picked with a chance in proportion to its weight"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq6"}}}P then shades the kept candidate as if it had been drawn in proportion to the target.

But the candidate was not drawn that way.

So the shaded result is multiplied by a correction, ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.03}}
    "the average of all eight weights"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "ris-unbiased"}}}With that correction, RIS is unbiased, right on average, ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 8.0 16.0} {risStepRange 8.0 16.0}}
    "however many candidates it draws"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.2 sentence}}}Shading as if drawn by the target means dividing by the kept candidate's target.

Together with the correction, that division makes a single number, called W.

So the light at P is ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.03 0.06}}
    "what the kept candidate brings, times W"}.

### Two samples, worked by hand {#c2-toy kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoToy panelValues {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    {toyStep 0.0}
    {toyDrop 0.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

Each part of W has a job, and a world small enough to work by hand shows it.

It has only two light samples, A and B, and it is the lab's example, not the paper's.

The cheap choice draws ${overText {' {toyStepRange 0.0 1.0}} "A three times in four, and B once"}.

But ${overText {' {toyStepRange 1.0 2.0}} "B brings three times as much light as A"}.

Here the target is exactly what each sample brings.

So the true total is four, one from A and three from B.

Dividing by the probability ${overText
    {' {toyStepRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "gives A a weight of four thirds, and B a weight of twelve"}.

B is drawn rarely, so its weight is large when it does turn up.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq6"}}}Now RIS draws two candidates, and keeps one.

${overText {' {toyStepRange 3.0 4.0}} "Two A's come up nine times in sixteen"}, and the estimate is then four thirds.

${overText {' {toyStepRange 4.0 5.0}} "One A and one B come up six times in sixteen"}, and the estimate is then six and two thirds.

It is the same whichever one is kept, because the target here equals what each sample brings.

${overText {' {toyStepRange 5.0 6.0}} "Two B's come up once in sixteen"}, and the estimate is then twelve.

No single estimate is four.

But ${overText
    {' {toyStepRange 6.0 7.0}}
    "averaged with these chances, the estimates come to exactly four"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "ris-unbiased"}}}That is what unbiased means, correct on average, though not in every run.

${overText {' {toyStepRange 7.0 8.0}} "W does one more job"}.

On average, it is one over the chance that RIS keeps the sample.

RIS keeps B four times in ten, and when it does, W averages two and a half.

${cue "key" {' {toyDrop 2.0}}}Without the average of the weights, every estimate is one, far below four.

That average estimates the target's total, four here, and so it puts back the scale.

${cue "key" {' {toyDrop 1.0}}}Without dividing by the probability, the estimates average one and a half.

The rarely drawn B then counts for far too little.

${cue "key" {' {toyDrop 0.0}}}So W needs both, ${formulaSaid f"{W = \frac{\sum w}{M\, \hat{p}(y)}}" "the sum of the weights, over the count M, over the kept candidate's target"
    {'}}.

With only two candidates, the estimates still spread from four thirds to twelve.

### More candidates, less noise {#c2-ris-more kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReservoirs panelPerformance {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {noReuse
    1.0}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "ris-converges"}}}${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 1.0 8.0}}
    "The more candidates a pixel draws"}, the more closely its kept candidate follows the target.

So the noise in the room falls ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 8.0 32.0}}
    "as the count rises to thirty-two"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 26.0 sentence}}}But ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 32.0 64.0}}
    "the cost rises too"}, because every candidate has to be weighted.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 18.0 sentence}}}${cue "key" {' {restirView 4.0}}}And the target ignores shadows, so a kept candidate may still be hidden from P.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 0.0}}}RIS with these candidates is ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.04}}
    "the first pass of ReSTIR"}.

Every pixel runs it.

### Keeping one from a stream {#c2-wrs kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRis panelCode {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {append
    {noReuse 8.0}
    {' listingRis {highlightCode "ris" {' "candidate"}} {restirSeed 5.0} {risStep 0.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

RIS as described has one more problem, its storage.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}RIS keeps ${overText
    {' {risStepRange 0.0 8.0}}
    "every candidate"} until it picks one.

A GPU cannot afford ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 8.0 32.0} {risStepRange 8.0 32.0}}
    "such a list for every pixel"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "wrs"}}}Weighted reservoir sampling picks one item from a stream, a sequence that arrives one item at a time and is seen only once.

${cue "key" {' {callout "alg2"}}}${cue "key" {' {restirCandidates 8.0} {risStep 0.0}}}It keeps a small record of three things, ${overText
    {' {risStepRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "the kept item, the running sum of weights, and the count of items so far"}.

${cue "key" {' {risStep 0.0}}}This record is the reservoir from chapter one.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "ris" {' "update"}}}}When ${overText
    {' {risStepRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "P's first candidate arrives"}, the reservoir keeps it.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq9"}}}${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "ris" {' "keep"}}}}Each later candidate ${overText
    {' {risStepRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "first adds its weight to the sum"}.

It then ${overText {' {risStepRange 2.0 3.0}} "replaces the kept one"} with a chance equal to its own weight divided by that new sum.

${overText {' {risStepRange 3.0 8.0}} "One by one, all eight candidates pass through the reservoir"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "ris" {' "finish"}}}}At the end, each candidate has been kept ${overText
    {' {risPickRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "with a chance in proportion to its weight"}, exactly as RIS needs.

${cue "key" {' {callout "combine"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.3 sentence}}}And two reservoirs can be merged into one, ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 8.0 16.0} {risStepRange 8.0 16.0}}
    "as if both their streams had passed through a single reservoir"}.

That merge is what will let pixels share.

### Where to read it {#c2-sources kind=2d sources="many-lights, hierarchies, tiled, path-reuse, denoisers, eq3, eq4, eq5, eq6, ris-unbiased, ris-converges, wrs, eq9, alg2"}

The related work is in section seven, and these two ideas are in section two.

## Streaming RIS {#c3 number=3 target=116 summary="RIS through one reservoir per pixel: what it stores, the paper's choices, and what it costs." sources="alg3, storage, candidates-param, alias, target-param, streaming-cost"}

### One reservoir per pixel {#c3-alg3 kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRis panelCode {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {append
    {noReuse 8.0}
    {' listingRis {highlightCode "ris" {' "candidate"}} {restirSeed 5.0} {risStep 0.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {callout "alg3"}}}${overText {' {risStepRange 0.0 1.0}} "Put the two ideas together"}, and you have streaming RIS, the first pass of every frame.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "ris" {' "weight"}}}}Each pixel ${overText
    {' {risStepRange 1.0 8.0}}
    "streams its candidates through one reservoir"}, and keeps only the reservoir.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "ris" {' "finish"}}}}When the stream ends, the pixel turns the sum of weights into W, the number from chapter two.

${overText {' {restirCandidatesRange 8.0 12.0} {risStepRange 8.0 12.0}} "W is"} ${formulaSaid f"{W = \frac{\sum w}{M\, \hat{p}(y)}}" "the average weight, the sum over the count M, divided by the kept candidate's target"
    {'}}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "ris" {' "shade"}}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}Then the pixel is shaded with ${overText
    {' {risPickRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "what its kept candidate brings"}, times W.

### What a reservoir holds {#c3-store kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReservoirs panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {append
    {noReuse 32.0}
    {' {restirView 1.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "storage"}}}So at the end, a reservoir holds only three numbers.

They are ${overText {' {restirViewRange 1.0 3.0}} "the sample y it kept, the count M, and W"}.

W takes the place of the running sum, which is no longer needed.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 1.0}}}This view shows y, ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.04}}
    "colouring each pixel by the light its sample is on"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 26.0 sentence}}}Neighbouring pixels on one surface ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.04 0.08}}
    "often keep the same lights"}, because those lights matter to all of them.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 3.0}}}This view shows W instead, which is larger ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.08 0.12}}
    "where the kept sample was unlikely to be drawn"}.

A larger W lets that rare sample count for more.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 2.0}}}And this one shows M, thirty-two in every lit pixel, ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 32.0 64.0}}
    "one for each candidate it drew"}.

### The paper's choices {#c3-params kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRis panelHidden {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {append
    {noReuse 8.0}
    {' {restirSeed 5.0} {risStep 8.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {callout "candidates-param"}}}${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 8.0 32.0} {risStepRange 8.0 32.0}}
    "Those thirty-two candidates"} are the paper's choice.

${cue "key" {' {callout "alias"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 24.0 sentence}}}The paper's lights are emissive triangles.

It picks ${overText {' {restirSeedRange 5.0 9.0}} "each candidate's triangle by power"}, from an alias table, a lookup table that draws by weight.

Then it picks a uniform point on that triangle.

${overText {' {restirSeedRange 9.0 13.0}} "The lab's lights are spheres instead, picked by power"}
with a table of summed powers and a binary search.

This gives ${overText {' {restirSeedRange 13.0 17.0}} "the same distribution"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "target-param"}}}${cue "key" {' {risWeigh 0.0}}}Next, the paper's target is ${overText
    {' {risWeighRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "the light a candidate would bring"} if nothing stood in the way.

${cue "key" {' {callout "streaming-cost"}}}Streaming makes the storage constant, but ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 32.0 64.0} {risStepRange 32.0 64.0}}
    "the work still grows with every candidate"}.

So instead of drawing more candidates, the rest of the method reuses the candidates that other pixels and earlier frames drew.

### Where to read it {#c3-sources kind=2d sources="alg3, storage, candidates-param, alias, target-param, streaming-cost"}

Streaming RIS is in section three point one, and the paper's choices are in section five.

## Spatial reuse {#c4 number=4 target=190 summary="Combining reservoirs, the neighbours each pixel combines, the rounds that multiply its candidates, and the test that rejects some." sources="combine, reweight, weight-meaning, alg4, neighbors-param, spatial, spatial-passes, geometry-test"}

### Combining two reservoirs {#c4-combine kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpatial panelCode {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {'
    listingCombine
    {highlightCode "combine" {' "update"}}
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 1.0}
    {restirNeighbours 1.0}
    {spatialStep 0.0}
    {restirSeed 5.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

After the first pass, P holds one reservoir, and so does ${overText
    {' {restirNeighboursRange 1.0 5.0}}
    "each of its neighbours"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "combine"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}To combine two reservoirs, the method ${overText
    {' {spatialStepRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "treats each one's kept sample as a single new candidate"}.

A new candidate needs a weight, its target over the chance it was drawn, as in chapter two.

For a neighbour's sample, that chance is the chance that the neighbour's reservoir kept it.

No one recorded that chance.

${cue "key" {' {callout "weight-meaning"}}}But on average, that reservoir's W is one over it, as the worked example showed.

So the target times W takes the place of the weight.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 22.0 sentence}}}${overText
    {' {pickURange 0.88 0.9}}
    "The target is taken at P"}, because a sample that suits a neighbour may not suit P.

Times M, P's own reservoir gets back exactly its sum of weights, because W divided that sum by M.

So a reservoir counts as much as all the candidates behind it.

${cue "key" {' {callout "reweight"}}}So a sample's weight is ${formulaSaid f"{\hat{p}_P(y) \cdot W \cdot M}" "the target at P, times its reservoir's W, times its M"
    {'}}.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}${overText
    {' {spatialStepRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The panel works it out for P's own reservoir and each neighbour's"}, factor by factor.

Here every reservoir drew thirty-two candidates, so M scales every weight alike, and changes nothing yet.

It matters in the next chapter, where the frame before can stand for far more.

${cue "key" {' panelCode}}${cue "key" {' {callout "alg4"}}}${cue "key" {'
    {highlightCode "combine" {' "count"}}}}The combined reservoir ${overText
        {' {spatialStepRange 2.0 3.0}}
        "keeps one of the samples, in proportion to these weights"}.

It stands for every candidate behind them, ${overText
    {' {spatialStepRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "so their counts add up"}.

Its W then comes out as in chapter two, from the sum of the weights and the new count.

### Neighbours in a disc {#c4-neighbours kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpatial panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {'
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 1.0}
    {restirNeighbours 1.0}
    {restirRadius 5.0}
    {restirSeed 5.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

P shares with a few neighbours at once, ${overText
    {' {restirNeighboursRange 1.0 3.0}}
    "and does so in rounds"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "neighbors-param"}}}In each round, P picks ${overText
    {' {restirNeighboursRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "k neighbours"} ${overText {' {restirRadiusRange 5.0 30.0}} "within thirty pixels"}, and the paper uses five in its biased version.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}The paper picks them with a low-discrepancy sequence, ${overText
    {' {restirSeedRange 5.0 9.0}}
    "points that look random but spread evenly"}.

It does so because fixed neighbours caused distracting artifacts.

The lab uses one such sequence, called R2.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 24.0 sentence}}}${cue "key" {' {spatialStep 0.0}}}Each line here runs from P to a neighbour, ${overText
    {' {spatialStepRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "and on to the sample that neighbour's reservoir holds"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.3 sentence}}}P ${overText
    {' {spatialStepRange 2.0 5.0}}
    "combines the reservoirs of all k neighbours"}.

Its count M grows ${overText
    {' {restirNeighboursRange 5.0 8.0} {spatialStepRange 5.0 8.0}}
    "by each neighbour's candidates"}.

### Rounds multiply the candidates {#c4-rounds kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReservoirs panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 0.0}
    {restirStage 3.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "spatial"}}}${overText
    {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "A single round already removes much of the noise"}.

${overText
    {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "A second round combines the reservoirs the first round made"}, so the candidates multiply.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 2.0}}}So after n rounds, a pixel can stand for ${formulaSaid f"{k^n M}" "k to the power n, times M"
    {'}} candidates, ${overText
        {' {restirNeighboursRange 5.0 8.0}}
        "if every round finds new neighbours"}.

This view shows M, now far larger than the thirty-two each pixel drew.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 0.0}}}${overText
    {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 2.0 4.0}}
    "The gain fades once the rounds have gathered the nearby candidates"}, because later rounds find the same ones again.

${cue "key" {' {callout "spatial-passes"}}}${overText
    {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 4.0 2.0}}
    "The paper uses two rounds"}, or ${overText
        {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 2.0 1.0} {restirUnbiasedRange 0.0 1.0}}
        "one in its unbiased version"}.

### Neighbours across an edge {#c4-test kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpatial panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {'
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 1.0}
    {restirNeighbours 4.0}
    {restirGeometryTest 0.0}
    {restirSeed 5.0}
    {pickU 0.56}
    {pickV 0.64}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}But a neighbour ${overText
    {' {restirNeighboursRange 4.0 12.0}}
    "across an object's edge"} sees different light from P.

${cue "key" {' {callout "geometry-test"}}}So the paper's biased version ${overText
    {' {restirGeometryTestRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "skips a neighbour"} whose depth differs from P's by more than ten percent.

It also skips one whose normal differs by more than twenty-five degrees.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 22.0 sentence}}}Here ${overText
    {' {pickVRange 0.64 0.66}}
    "the skipped neighbours are drawn in red"}.

${overText
    {' {restirGeometryTestRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "Without this test, those neighbours would pass on samples that suit P poorly"}.

The lab applies the same test ${overText
    {' {restirTemporalRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "to the pixel in the previous frame"}.

The next chapter reuses that pixel's reservoir.

### Where to read it {#c4-sources kind=2d sources="combine, reweight, weight-meaning, alg4, neighbors-param, spatial, spatial-passes, geometry-test"}

Spatial reuse is in section three point two, and its parameters are in section five.

## Temporal and visibility reuse {#c5 number=5 target=123 summary="Reusing the frame before, the clamp on its count, the moving lights, and the shadow ray before reuse." sources="temporal, temporal-pick, clamp, visibility, alg5"}

### The frame before {#c5-temporal kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReuse panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {'
    {restirSpatialPasses 0.0}
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirTemporalClamp 1.0}
    {pickV 0.52}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

Neighbours share across the screen, and ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.03}}
    "the previous frame can share too"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "temporal"}}}After each frame, every pixel's final reservoir, the one it was shaded with, is kept.

${cue "key" {' {callout "temporal-pick"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}In the next frame, each pixel ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.03 0.06}}
    "finds where its point was in the previous frame"}.

It then ${overText
    {' {restirTemporalRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "combines the reservoir kept there with its own"}, just as it combines a neighbour's.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 22.0 sentence}}}That kept reservoir was itself combined with the frame before it, so every past frame feeds the present one.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 2.0}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.2 sentence}}}Each combination adds the counts, ${overText
    {' {restirTemporalClampRange 1.0 20.0}}
    "so M climbs from frame to frame"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "clamp"}}}If nothing stops it, ${overText
    {' {restirTemporalClampRange 20.0 100.0}}
    "M grows without limit"}.

Then ${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.06 0.1}} "old samples would outweigh new ones"}.

${overText
    {' {restirTemporalClampRange 100.0 20.0}}
    "So the paper clamps the previous frame's M to at most twenty times this frame's"}.

### Lights on the move {#c5-dynamic kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReuse panelValues {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    {restirSpatialPasses 0.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.1}} "All this time, the lights keep moving"}.

In the lab, a sample is a light and a point on that light, ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.1 0.2}}
    "so the sample moves with its light"}.

Each time a sample is combined, ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.2 0.3}}
    "its target is evaluated again at the light's new position"}.

### A shadow ray before reuse {#c5-visibility kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReuse panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {'
    {restirSpatialPasses 0.0}
    {restirView 4.0}
    {restirStage 0.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}The target still ignores shadows, so ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.04}}
    "a kept sample may be hidden from its pixel"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "visibility"}}}So before any reuse, each pixel ${overText
    {' {restirStageRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "traces one shadow ray to the sample it kept"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "alg5"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 22.0 sentence}}}If the ray is blocked, ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.04 0.07}}
    "W becomes zero"}.

The reservoir still keeps M, the count of candidates it stands for.

In this view, ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.07 0.1}}
    "green pixels kept a visible sample, and red pixels a hidden one"}.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 0.0} {restirStage 3.0}}}A combined sample's weight is its target times W times M, so a hidden sample's weight is zero.

A sample with no weight ${overText
    {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "cannot spread to neighbouring pixels"}.

${overText {' {restirVisibilityReuseRange 1.0 0.0}} "Here the lab leaves the shadow ray out"}.

The shadows fill with noise, because neighbours pass on lights they cannot see.

### The whole frame {#c5-alg5 kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    {restirStage 0.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "alg5"}}}That completes ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.03}}
    "one frame of the paper's algorithm"}.

In order, each pixel draws candidates, ${overText
    {' {restirStageRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "tests the kept one's visibility"}, ${overText
        {' {restirStageRange 1.0 2.0}}
        "reuses the previous frame"}, ${overText
            {' {restirStageRange 2.0 3.0}}
            "reuses its neighbours"}, and is shaded.

### Where to read it {#c5-sources kind=2d sources="temporal, temporal-pick, clamp, visibility, alg5"}

Temporal and visibility reuse are in section three point two, and the clamp is in section five.

## Bias, and removing it {#c6 number=6 target=240 summary="Why reuse biases RIS, the paper's one-dimensional example, the weights that remove the bias, and the unbiased combination." sources="bias-source, eq12, weight-meaning, eq19, fig7, eq20, naive-m, eq22, talbot-cost, alg6, approx-pdf, unbiased-cost"}

### Every pixel has its own target {#c6-source kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpatial panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {'
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 1.0}
    {spatialStep 0.0}
    {restirSeed 5.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

The reuse so far has one flaw, and it comes from the neighbours.

${cue "key" {' {callout "bias-source"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}${overText
    {' {pickURange 0.88 0.85}}
    "Every pixel has its own target"}, because its surface faces its own way and sees its own lights.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 22.0 sentence}}}So ${overText
    {' {spatialStepRange 0.0 5.0}}
    "a sample from a neighbour"} was chosen for the neighbour's target, not for P's.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq12"}}}Recall what W does.

It stands in for one over the chance ${overText
    {' {restirSeedRange 5.0 7.0}}
    "that RIS keeps that sample"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "weight-meaning"}}}On average, W must equal one over that chance exactly, or the image comes out ${overText
    {' {lightIntensityRange 1.0 0.7}}
    "too bright or too dark"}.

But a neighbour's W was built for the neighbour's target, so for P it may be wrong.

### The paper's example in one dimension {#c6-1d kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBias panelValues {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    {biasWeight 0.0}
    {biasCandidates 2.0}
    {biasNearZero 0.0}
    {biasReveal 0.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "fig7"}}}The paper shows what goes wrong in one dimension.

${overText {' {biasRevealRange 0.0 1.0}} "Here the target is"} ${formulaSaid f"{\hat{p}(x) = 2 - 2x}" "a ramp that falls from two to zero"
    {'}}.

Half the candidates come from ${overText {' {biasRevealRange 1.0 2.0}} "a flat distribution"}, one that picks every place equally.

The other candidates come from ${overText {' {biasRevealRange 2.0 3.0}} "a step"}, a distribution that covers ${overText
    {' {biasMarkRange 0.05 0.45}}
    "only the left half of the range"}.

${overText
    {' {biasRevealRange 3.0 4.0} {biasCandidatesRange 2.0 4.0}}
    "The thick line shows what W should equal at each place"}.

It is one over the chance that RIS outputs that place, ${overText
    {' {biasCandidatesRange 4.0 6.0}}
    "measured over many runs"}.

${overText
    {' {biasRevealRange 4.0 5.0} {biasCandidatesRange 6.0 10.0}}
    "The thin line shows what W really averages at each place"}.

If W is correct, the two lines match.

On the left half, ${overText {' {biasMarkRange 0.05 0.45}} "they agree"}.

On the right half, ${overText
    {' {biasMarkRange 0.45 0.9}}
    "only the flat candidates can produce a sample"}.

Yet W still divides by ${overText {' {biasMarkRange 0.9 0.6}} "the count of every candidate"}, the step ones included.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq19"}}}${overText
    {' {biasCandidatesRange 10.0 20.0}}
    "So W comes out half as large as it should"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq19"}}}The paper writes this as ${formulaSaid f"{E[W \mid y] = \frac{1}{p(y)} \cdot \frac{|Z(y)|}{M}}" "on average, W is one over the chance, times the share of the candidates that could produce the sample"
    {'}}.

${overText
    {' {biasMarkRange 0.6 0.75}}
    "At the marker, half the candidates could produce the sample"}, so W averages half of one over the chance.

An image would then come out ${overText {' {lightIntensityRange 1.0 0.6}} "too dark"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "approx-pdf"}}}In ReSTIR, each reservoir a pixel combines acts like the step, ${overText
    {' {biasMarkRange 0.6 0.2}}
    "a source that covers only some of the places"}.

A neighbour whose surface faces away from a light, or whose view of it is blocked, cannot produce samples of that light that P can use.

### Counting only those that could {#c6-fix kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBias panelValues {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    {biasWeight 0.0}
    {biasCandidates 4.0}
    {biasNearZero 0.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq20"}}}The fix is to divide only by ${overText
    {' {biasMarkRange 0.55 0.9}}
    "the count of the candidates that could have produced the sample"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "naive-m"}}}${overText
    {' {biasWeightRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "With that count, the two lines agree"}.

At the marker, W now divides by two, the candidates that could, not by all four.

${overText
    {' {biasNearZeroRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Now let the step be nearly zero on the right half, instead of exactly zero"}.

Then its candidates still count there, and ${overText
    {' {biasCandidatesRange 4.0 10.0}}
    "W becomes very noisy"}.

That is because ${overText {' {biasMarkRange 0.9 0.6}} "a step candidate on the right half"} has a tiny probability there, and so a huge weight.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq22"}}}${cue "key" {' {biasMark -1.0}}}${overText
    {' {biasWeightRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The paper's better weight asks, for each candidate, how likely its own distribution was to produce the sample"}.

It divides that chance by the sum of the chances of all the distributions.

This weight is the balance heuristic, from multiple importance sampling, the mix of ways to draw samples from chapter two.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 26.0 sentence}}}With it, the two lines agree, and ${overText
    {' {biasCandidatesRange 10.0 14.0}}
    "W stays steady"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "talbot-cost"}}}The original RIS, by Talbot and colleagues, uses another weight here, the target divided by the sum of every distribution's probability.

When every candidate has its own distribution, each candidate's weight needs every distribution's probability.

So that weight's cost grows with the square of ${overText
    {' {biasCandidatesRange 14.0 20.0}}
    "the number of candidates"}.

The paper's weight is needed only for the sample that is kept, so its cost grows only with the number of candidates.

### The unbiased combination {#c6-alg6 kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpatial panelCode {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {'
    listingCombine
    {highlightCode "combine" {' "unbiased"}}
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 1.0}
    {restirUnbiased 1.0}
    {spatialStep 0.0}
    {restirSeed 5.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {callout "alg6"}}}${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "combine" {' "z"}}}}Back in the room, the paper's unbiased combination, its algorithm six, uses the first fix, the count.

For each reservoir, ${overText {' {spatialStepRange 0.0 5.0}} "P's and its neighbours'"}, it checks that pixel's target at the sample.

It adds up the counts M ${overText
    {' {restirNeighboursRange 5.0 8.0} {spatialStepRange 5.0 8.0}}
    "of only the reservoirs whose target there is not zero"}.

Those are the reservoirs that could have produced the sample.

The paper says a version with the balance heuristic is unbiased in the same way.

${cue "key" {' {callout "unbiased-cost"}}}With visibility reuse, a neighbour's target includes ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.04}}
    "whether the neighbour can see the sample"}, so checking it costs ${overText
        {' {restirNeighboursRange 8.0 12.0} {spatialStepRange 8.0 12.0}}
        "one extra shadow ray per neighbour"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "biased-mode"}}}${overText
    {' {restirUnbiasedRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "The biased version skips that cost"}.

Instead, it darkens the edges of objects a little.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 26.0 sentence}}}The paper offers both versions, and the lab offers both in its dock, the panel of controls.

### Where to read it {#c6-sources kind=2d sources="bias-source, eq12, weight-meaning, eq19, fig7, eq20, naive-m, eq22, talbot-cost, alg6, approx-pdf, unbiased-cost"}

Bias and its removal are in section four, and the example is figure seven.

## Experiments on this machine {#c7 number=7 target=102 summary="The paper's comparisons, run on this machine as the video is recorded." sources="still-images, results-setup, biased-vs-unbiased, sample-count"}

### The comparisons {#c7-methods kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoMethods panelPerformance {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {append
    {noReuse 1.0}
    {' {restirStage 3.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

With every part explained, it is time for the paper's comparisons.

These are the experimentation data run on this current machine, the one that recorded this video.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 24.0 sentence}}}The panel on the right measures each pass on this machine, so you can see ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 1.0 8.0}}
    "each pass's time"} as these frames render.

${cue "key" {' {callout "results-setup"}}}First comes ${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 8.0 32.0}}
    "streaming RIS alone"}, the baseline the paper compares against.

${cue "key" {' {callout "still-images"}}}${overText
    {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "Next, spatial reuse joins it"}.

The paper offers this version for still images, which have no previous frame.

${overText {' {restirTemporalRange 0.0 1.0}} "Then temporal reuse joins too"}.

This gives the full method in its biased version.

One setting differs from the paper.

The paper's biased results keep four reservoirs in each pixel, and the lab keeps one.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.05}}
    "Watch how much noise disappears"}.

Then look at the panel, for ${overText
    {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "how little time the new passes add"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "biased-vs-unbiased"}}}${overText
    {' {restirUnbiasedRange 0.0 1.0} {restirSpatialPassesRange 3.0 1.0}}
    "Last comes the unbiased version"}, with its extra shadow rays in the spatial pass.

The paper reports that ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.05 0.1}}
    "the biased version has less noise than the unbiased one"}.

It also reports that the unbiased version loses no brightness to bias, so its edges are not darkened.

### Against a reference {#c7-reference kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReference panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    {restirAccumulate 0.0}
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 0.0}}}}}}

Each version estimates the same image.

Here the lab makes that image ${overText
    {' {restirAccumulateRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "by averaging the room over many frames of streaming RIS"}, until the average stops changing.

Streaming RIS is unbiased, so this average has no bias, and enough frames leave almost no noise.

This image is called the reference.

The reference also lets each version be measured.

An experiment that comes with this video measures how far each version's image is from the reference, on whichever machine runs it.

### Where to read it {#c7-sources kind=2d sources="still-images, results-setup, biased-vs-unbiased, sample-count"}

The paper's own results are in section six.

## Conclusion {#c8 number=8 target=90 summary="The frame once more, the paper's conclusion and limits, three takeaways, and the references." sources="conclusion, limitations, screen-space"}

### The frame once more {#c8-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {noReuse 1.0}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

To close, here is one frame once more, step by step.

${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 1.0 32.0}}
    "Each pixel draws thirty-two candidates, and keeps one in its reservoir"}.

${overText
    {' {restirStageRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Each pixel then traces a shadow ray to the sample it kept"}.

${overText
    {' {restirTemporalRange 0.0 1.0} {restirStageRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "It combines the previous frame's reservoir with its own"}.

${overText
    {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 0.0 2.0} {restirStageRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "Then it combines its neighbours' reservoirs, round by round"}.

Only then is the pixel shaded, ${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.03}} "with its final reservoir"}.

### What the paper concludes {#c8-paper kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 0.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "conclusion"}}}The authors conclude that ${overText
    {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "filtering need not come only after rendering"}, as a denoiser does it.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 1.0}}}ReSTIR already filters while it samples, but what it filters is ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.04}}
    "the chance of picking each light"}, not the colours.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 0.0}}}A denoiser can still run afterwards, on its better samples.

${cue "key" {' {callout "limitations"}}}ReSTIR's gain shrinks where neighbouring pixels differ, because then their samples help each other less.

Neighbouring pixels differ most where hidden surfaces come into view, where the lighting changes sharply, and ${overText
    {' {lightPhaseRange 0.04 0.2}}
    "where lights move fast"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "screen-space"}}}ReSTIR also works only at the first surface each pixel sees.

The authors leave the surfaces beyond it to future work.

### Three things to remember {#c8-takeaways kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {append
    {noReuse 1.0}
    {' {restirStage 3.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${overText
    {' {restirCandidatesRange 1.0 32.0}}
    "First, a reservoir sums up a whole stream of candidates in a few numbers"}.

${overText
    {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 0.0 2.0} {restirTemporalRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Second, reuse multiplies the candidates behind each pixel"}
for the cost of only a few shadow rays.

${overText
    {' {restirUnbiasedRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Third, reuse stays unbiased only when W counts every reservoir that could have produced a sample"}.

